    "Cost per document is high relative to digital methods (travel time, copy fees, runner labor), commonly tens of dollars per retrieved document depending on office and distance (estimate).",
    "Monetized as a premium 'hard-to-get record' service layered on top of broader court/registry data products, or sold directly as a retrieval service to law firms and title companies.",
    "Moat is strong and durable: a maintained nationwide runner network with per-office relationships takes years to build and is genuinely hard for a new entrant to replicate quickly.",
    "Volume economics favor consolidating requests by geography (batch several document pulls into one office visit) to amortize the fixed cost of a runner trip.",
    "Build-vs-buy: most data businesses contract an existing retrieval-service network rather than build runner coverage themselves, given the low utilization of any single office relationship.",
